hello everybody  i'm richard chen from nyu  and i'm going to talk to you today about  orchestrating the brain  for  social behavior  we have some ambitious questions and  goals facing us today  first  to delineate the goals of studies of the  brain  computation and behavior  and ask how they can be integrated with  each other  to describe how  oxytocin and other neuromodulators  transform circuits in the brain  and actually operate at a different  level than fast neurotransmitters  and finally to  take a more meta view of the whole  problem and evaluate the synergies and  challenges  of multi-level multi-investigator  projects  asking the question of how success in  these projects can be defined  it just so happens  that in this day and age of  pandemic  sheltering  two bucks crossed my desk in the last  week  one was a copy of principles of neural  science  the kandel textbook  to which i contributed a chapter  and secondly a book called noise by  nobel prize winning daniel kahneman and  his colleagues  these are two very different approaches  to  function of the brain  principles  is all about years of hard won knowledge  written about by many experts noise is a  much more popular oriented book trying  to describe how human judgment is  fallible  and subject to large amounts of  variation  both bias and variability and the point  is that if we could reduce that  variability  we would be much better off  whether we're trying to fight the  pandemic or simply buy a used car  so  my recollection of these problems goes  back to the early days of being a  graduate student and listening to a  marvelous lecture by colin blakemore  which mentioned  rene descartes  models of vision  rene descartes knew  that an object in the real world  got turned upside down  in terms of its image on the retiny  and he was worried about the fact that  this image was upside down  so he arranged the optic nerve  and its connections to  what he thought was the seat of  conscience consciousness the pineal  gland  by having  a reinversion of that image  so the image would project properly  upright onto the pineal gland  even as a graduate student i realized  that descartes was simply kicking the  problem down the road  because how did the pineal gland know  what to make of the uh  the image  and  was  the uh reinversion by  the nervous system in answer to the  question  a much more fundamental question of how  we saw  descartes had  equally metaphorical ways of looking at  memory  he saw memory  as the imprint produced by a set of  needles  that could make little holes in a piece  of canvas  and when the canvas was stretched out  the holes would become apparent  and the memory could be recalled  so this is a metaphorical view both the  vision  and of memory  and as it turns out these views these  could be thought of as a hypothesis for  how our brain processes information  stores it and recalls it  now the topic for today  is about oxytocin  this is a very interesting  chemical a nine amino acid peptide  produced in the hypothalamus of the  brain and release centrally and in the  periphery for example to  trigger the milk milk letdown reflex  oxytocin  consisting of nine amino acids is  conveniently shaped like the letter nine  an easy way to remember that  and  it's best known for its role  in  production of milk and childbirth so  it's really important for reproduction  but it's also implicated in many social  behaviors  social encounters  between people and between humans and  animals  cause an increase in oxytocin that could  be measured within a few minutes  so this is something that is highly  relevant to social behavior  oxytocin and its cousin vasopressin  signaling was shown to be important for  social behavior in rodents in prairie  voles and mountain bowls which differ  radically in the amount of oxytocin  receptor that they express in classic  experiments of larry young and tom insel  oxytocin has also been  tested as a possible clinical agent for  treatment of autism although the jury is  still out on this matter  so oxytocin really exemplifies the logic  of neuromodulation  the central nervous system uses fast  transmitters for example glutamate the  excitatory transmitter and acetylcholine  but it also has many neuromodulators  loosely defined as slower and less  directly acting agents which can be  peptides or even lipids and also small  molecules like glutamate and gaba  studying these molecules is extra  challenging because we not only need to  understand the neuromodulator how it's  produced and how it's act how it acts  but we also need to understand the basic  circuit that it acts upon  our hypothesis is  that neural circuits function at  multiple levels and they can be  transformed to have a different  personality  by the state of the neuromodulator  this idea was put forward for  invertebrate circuits for example in the  lobster by eve martyr and others but  it's never been fully worked out in  vertebrates and this whole idea of  modulating the existing operation of a  circuit and turning it into a different  kind of circuit is both powerful in  concept and has interes interesting  evolutionary aspects  oxytocin is a particularly interesting  example  and our goal today is to derive  principles  generalizable not only to  oxytocin but generalizable to other  neuromodulators and circuits  now in the brain  the receptors for oxytocin couple to  downstream g proteins  and the receptors are found unevenly  across the brain  one area where it's particularly  prominent is in the hippocampus in a  particular subfield of the hippocampus  called ca2  you can see that  in  this diagram which shows ca2 principal  neurons pyramidal cells  lit up because they're expressing  td tomato under the control of an  oxytocin receptor promoter  this indicates that ca2 is much richer  in oxytocin receptors than its  neighboring and more famous subregion  called ca1  the  oxytocin receptors in ca2 are  particularly interesting  because  they have been implicated in social  behavior in work from steve siegelbaum's  lab  and also  raman sahe showed that removing oxytocin  receptors from these neurons caused a  loss of social recognition  memory  driven by oxytocin was abolished  furthermore  oxytocin receptors are also present in a  particular  small but very interesting region of the  brain  called the ventral medial portion of the  hypothalamus  and the ventrolateral part of that so  these two small regions are very  important in diulin's work for  controlling aggression and they also  happen to be replete with oxytocin  receptors  so we obtained funding from nih uh uh  in the mechanism called the u19  and my principal investigators uh our  co-investigators are rob fromkey yuri  birjaki moses ciao  annette gray  daiyoulin  adamar and elisa circus and we're all at  nyu  in the neuroscience institute and in the  scurbal institute  we are the only u19 group of all the  ones that have been funded that are  working on modulation  and our institutional organization  favors collaboration between us  a special thanks to our all-star cast of  external advisors and to the nih  personnel listed on the first slide who  are both our advocates critics and  teachers  our  year 19 grant which is being presented  here is an example of a successful  large science collaboration  chose to focus on a number of regions of  the brain that express the oxytocin  receptor in abundance  oxytocin is shown here being released  from the hypothalamus  and it acts on the auditory cortex the  vm-hvl the hippocampus and the lateral  septum  and  as a result of the actions on the  auditory cortex  you can see in the upper right hand  corner effects on pup retrieval  as a result of actions on the vm hvl  also in the hypothalamus we see changes  in aggression but also changes in the  recognition of one's social rank  so we have taken a multi-disciplinary  approach with this anatomical backdrop  looking at synaptic plasticity in the  auditory cortex  looking at the transfer of information  from hippocampus to the lateral septum  looking at the  physiology of how ca2 principal cells  respond to oxytocin and use various g  proteins like gi and go to control their  ion channels and therefore their  membrane potential and cellular  excitability  and finally  looking at the  ventromedial hypothalamus  where certain oxytocin receptor  responding cells are really important  for  putting one  self in the totem pole of society  and of course these four main projects  are  very well supported by  cores that are involved in producing  antibodies to the oxytocin receptor in  doing behavioral studies  in managing data and sharing data and  finally in administration and  coordination headed by heather mckeller  so our principal investigators have many  years of collecting collective  experience among them  and here you see some of the papers we  published  just over the last period of funding  transformation of a spatial map across  the hippocampal lateral septal circuit  transformation of the firing pattern of  ca2 pyramidal cells  sexual dimorphic  aggressive behaviors  and neuromodulation of maternal circuits  by oxytocin  so i'm going to tell you about some very  exciting work  showing the involvement of oxytocin  neurons  in enabling  the social transmission of maternal  behavior  this is beautiful work coming from iwana  garcia now a junior faculty member at  rutgers  working with robert frumke one of our  principal investigators  so now i'm going to  show  a video  showing the behavior of a experienced  mother who will come and retrieve her  pup  so you will see  her leave the  nest detect the pup  who is giving out calls of distress  grab it by the scruff of the neck and  take it right back to the nest  let's contrast that with the behavior of  a female mouse that is not given birth  and is inexperienced  and you will notice  you will notice  that the inexperienced mouse  really pays the pup no mind  uh ignores its calls  and will just casually walk right past  it  without doing anything  now  this is the behavior whereby  the inexperienced animal becomes  experienced  with the tutelage  of the mother  who has a vested interest in getting  some babysitting help  by teaching the naive female  how to go back to the nest  and how to gather pups  so in this behavior  called shepherding  fromkey's lab  noticed through 24 7 recordings  that mothers  would shepherd  inexperienced females back to the nest  time after time after time and they  recorded  they recorded literally hundreds of  these events  now notably when the shepherding is  occurring  the oxytocin neurons in the  inexperienced neuron in inexperienced  female fire off madly and help it to  acquire the skills that it needs  so  this social transmission of maternal  behavior  consists first of all  of oxytocin neurons firing in the virgin  that is benefiting from the shepherding  that firing is critical for the  acquisition of retrieval skills  because if you  block the oxytocin neurons from firing  the retrieval skills are not gained  the block of oxytocin receptors prevents  the learning  and  mimicry  of the firing of oxytocin neurons  actually helps promote the learning  so  the pharmacological block of the  oxytocin receptors by a very specific  antagonist prevents the acquisition of  behavior  so this shows in a very dramatic way the  involvement of the oxytocin resistor  receptor system in the social  transmission of maternal behavior  so the practical strategies of our group  are  to combine top-down and bottom-up  approaches  24 7 recordings of analog behavior on  one hand  analysis of oxytocin receptor  distribution signaling and  electrophysiological effects on the  other hand  we do both of these approaches within  individual labs our labs are pretty  broadly spread within themselves and we  collaborate together  the behavior proceeds hand in hand with  mechanistic analysis  and it also draws upon advances in labs  outside of the u19 project  for example our colleague yulong lee  working in beijing has done an excellent  job of creating receptors for oxytocin  or  optical receptors for oxytocin that can  monitor the local oxytocin concentration  we capitalize on our close physical  proximity to each other all within a  large superblock of hospital  we synergize with other u19 groups and  other eu mechanisms for example groups  that are studying the role of  oscillations in behavior in primates  other groups that are interested in  synaptic plasticity  and the role of  brain oscillations  we combine experiments with computations  in theory  and we try to push technological  advances for example yuri berjaki has  been very active in  studying  ways of recording from hundreds of  thousands of neurons  and rob frumpke's lab is pioneering the  use of caged oxytocin  and we finally we use grant and paper  writing as a way of training our diverse  trainees  for example  my lab  has  many folks who are at the interface  between our project which is more about  the biophysics and circuit mechanisms  and we collaborate together with ishmael  xinjing is involved in that with rob  propane's lab we collaborate with the  bhajaki lab  via  his colleague we collaborate with  daiyulin's lab studying both female  aggression and social hierarchy we  collaborate with  paul glimpser's lab  kenway  louis and beau shen  and finally we are actively involved in  working with the oxytocin antibody core  and the behavioral optogenetics core so  all of these collaborations are driven  by trainees the trainees are very  diverse and we're very proud of their  ability to progress for example bianca  jones marlin got a job at columbia  katie eyring  won an f-99 k f99k00 and is now working  with dan geshwin at ucla  so to close  we have been  successful  in publishing a number of papers  including papers about plasticity in the  auditory cortex  papers about  the amygdala and its role in regulating  aggressive and sexual behavior  papers about the routing of hippocampal  oscillations to the lateral septum  and yuri the co-author of this  recently won the gerard prize of the  society for third of neuroscience  and finally my lab is interested in  molecular and cellular mechanisms and we  study how the synaptic plasticity might  be supported by synaptic vesicle traffic  so that ends part one  and if there were time i would go on to  part two but i think i'm going to call  it a day for today  and simply end the presentation here  simply to add that terry sinowski's  famous diagram about the many levels of  brain investigation stand as a challenge  to us whether we are following the  footsteps of renee descartes or not  and part of what we need to do  is to take the abundance of genetic data  on devastating disease  and  use it to help us understand how neurons  work as individuals how synapses  communicate because many of the genes  and code proteins involved in the action  of individual neurons are indeed  individual synapses  only now are we beginning to comprehend  how these genes and gene products are  orchestrated to guide behavior  and we believe that circuits are a  critical intermediate between cellular  and molecular neuroscience reaching down  to the level of genes and proteins and  systems neuroscience are  ranging all the way up to the brain uh  and uh and behavior  so thanks very much for uh listening to  me and i look forward to any questions  that you might send me my email address  is richard.tsien  at  nyumc.org thanks again

